  f241: { lead: 'Each of the four countries has its own patron saint and its own day. Learn them as four pairs rather than eight separate facts — the saint and the date always come up together.' },
  f242: { lead: 'St Andrew was one of the apostles, and his diagonal cross is the origin of the Scottish saltire. The flag and the saint are the same fact seen from two directions.' },
  f243: { lead: 'St David is the only one of the four who was actually from the country he is patron of. That makes him easy to remember, and 1 March is the earliest of the four dates.' },
  f244: { lead: 'St Patrick is patron of the whole island of Ireland, not just Northern Ireland — which is why his day is celebrated on both sides of the border and around the world.' },
  f245: { lead: '1 March, and it is the first of the four in the calendar. The order through the year runs David, Patrick, George, Andrew — March, March, April, November.' },
  f246: { lead: '17 March. Along with St Andrew\'s Day it is an official holiday, which is a detail the handbook draws attention to and questions like to test.' },
  f247: { lead: '23 April, and notably *not* a public holiday in England — which is the point of the question about which patron saints\' days are official holidays.' },
  f248: { lead: '30 November, the last of the four. It is an official holiday in Scotland, pairing with St Patrick\'s Day in Northern Ireland.' },
  f249: { lead: 'Only two of the four. England and Wales do not get a holiday for their patron saints, which surprises people and is exactly why it is asked.' },
  f250: { lead: 'Gaelic survives mainly in the Highlands and Islands — the parts of Scotland furthest from English influence. It is a Celtic language, related to Irish rather than to English.' },
  f251: { lead: 'Ulster Scots sits alongside Irish Gaelic in Northern Ireland. The question usually asks you to name the *other* one, so knowing there are two is half the answer.' },
  f252: { lead: 'Cornish is Celtic, closer to Welsh and Breton than to English. Its inclusion makes the point that Britain\'s Celtic languages were never confined to Scotland, Wales and Ireland.' },
  f253: { lead: 'The handbook\'s population table is worth glancing at as a shape rather than a list: about 40 million in 1901, 50 million by 1951, and roughly 67 million now. England holds 84% of it.' },
