  f038: { lead: 'King John was forced into it by his barons — he did not offer it. That is the point: Magna Carta established that even a king is subject to law, because a king had to be made to accept it.' },
  f039: { lead: 'Runnymede, a meadow by the Thames. The place is asked about surprisingly often, so it is worth attaching to the date.' },
  f040: { lead: 'Magna Carta is simply Latin for "the Great Charter". Knowing the translation makes the term much easier to hold.' },
  f041: { lead: 'The Pale was the area around Dublin under English control. "Beyond the pale" as a phrase comes from exactly this — outside the boundary, and so outside civilised rule.' },
  f042: { lead: 'The Statute of Rhuddlan in 1284 annexed Wales to England. This is why Wales has no emblem on the Union Flag: it had already been absorbed centuries before the flag existed.' },
